Step 1: What is the root cause
The root cause is in the `Media.merge` static method in `django/forms/widgets.py`. The current implementation uses a "last insert index" strategy to merge two lists while preserving relative order. Specifically, it iterates through `list_2` in reverse and inserts new items at `last_insert_index` (which starts at `len(list_1)`). This approach assumes that if an item from `list_2` is not in `list_1`, it should be appended after all items from `list_1` that have been processed so far. However, when merging multiple media objects (e.g., `A + B + C`), the intermediate result of `A + B` may impose an arbitrary ordering constraint on items that are independent. For example, if `A` has `['color-picker.js']` and `B` has `['text-editor.js']`, the merge results in `['color-picker.js', 'text-editor.js']`. When this is merged with `C` (which has `['text-editor.js', 'text-editor-extras.js', 'color-picker.js']`), the algorithm detects that `text-editor-extras.js` (from C) is being inserted relative to `text-editor.js` and `color-picker.js` in a way that conflicts with the order established in the intermediate merge, even though `color-picker.js` and `text-editor.js` are independent. The warning is triggered because the algorithm incorrectly interprets the relative positions of independent items as a conflict. The core issue is that the merge algorithm does not correctly handle the transitive nature of ordering constraints when merging more than two lists, leading to false positives for `MediaOrderConflictWarning`.

Step 2: What exact changes to make
